{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794685911,"nonce":"7n3L8JScmBxMAwSMusTClDin3bI=","title":"The glyph atlas keeps one bitmap for every size within half a pixel, whichever was asked for first","message":"GlyphKey::size_key in blinc_text's atlas, and the two glyph caches in renderer.rs, are round(font_size * 2). A glyph asked for at 16.56 px and at 16.5 px is one entry, rasterized at whichever size reached it first. So the bitmap for a size depends on what was asked for before it, and text scaled to a size off the half-pixel step is drawn from a bitmap up to a quarter pixel off.\n\nSeen as a 3-level difference on the edge pixels of one scanline between two rendering paths that asked for the sizes of a scale animation in a different order (the node table against a full paint, in animation_paths). The paths' glyph primitives were field for field identical. The work on 879382f works around it: while a scale changes the raster size is moved to the half-pixel step, so every path asks for the step itself, at the cost of a bitmap up to 2% off 1:1 between steps (a CSS zoom reads 0.95 to 0.98 of native sharpness in flight). At rest it still asks for the exact size, which the atlas gives only if nothing else in that half pixel asked first.\n\nRoot fix to weigh: a finer key (an eighth of a pixel, say) in the three places, so each requested size gets its own bitmap. It would make the in-flight workaround unnecessary and the at-rest size exact. Costs: more atlas entries while a scale animates (about four times as many sizes), and any snapshot of text at sizes within the same half pixel changes. Check the goldens of blinc_recorder and the atlas unit tests that assume the half-pixel step.","files":null}]}